   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Hopfield Network: Recalling Noisy Letters\n",
    "\n",
    "A Hopfield network is a simple kind of memory. You store a few patterns in it, and later you can give it a damaged or noisy version of one of them and it tries to \"remember\" the original.\n",
    "\n",
    "In this notebook I:\n",
    "1. store a few letters (drawn on a 7x5 grid) in a Hopfield network\n",
    "2. add random noise to them\n",
    "3. let the network clean them up\n",
    "4. check how much noise it can handle and how many letters it can store before it gets confused"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "np.random.seed(7)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. The patterns\n",
    "\n",
    "Each letter is a 7 rows x 5 columns grid. `#` is a black pixel (+1) and `.` is a white pixel (-1). Hopfield networks work with +1 / -1 values instead of 1 / 0, which makes the maths cleaner."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "letters = {\n",
    "    \"A\": [\".###.\",\n",
    "          \"#...#\",\n",
    "          \"#...#\",\n",
    "          \"#####\",\n",
    "          \"#...#\",\n",
    "          \"#...#\",\n",
    "          \"#...#\"],\n",
    "    \"C\": [\".####\",\n",
    "          \"#....\",\n",
    "          \"#....\",\n",
    "          \"#....\",\n",
    "          \"#....\",\n",
    "          \"#....\",\n",
    "          \".####\"],\n",
    "    \"H\": [\"#...#\",\n",
    "          \"#...#\",\n",
    "          \"#...#\",\n",
    "          \"#####\",\n",
    "          \"#...#\",\n",
    "          \"#...#\",\n",
    "          \"#...#\"],\n",
    "    \"L\": [\"#....\",\n",
    "          \"#....\",\n",
    "          \"#....\",\n",
    "          \"#....\",\n",
    "          \"#....\",\n",
    "          \"#....\",\n",
    "          \"#####\"],\n",
    "    \"T\": [\"#####\",\n",
    "          \"..#..\",\n",
    "          \"..#..\",\n",
    "          \"..#..\",\n",
    "          \"..#..\",\n",
    "          \"..#..\",\n",
    "          \"..#..\"],\n",
    "    \"X\": [\"#...#\",\n",
    "          \"#...#\",\n",
    "          \".#.#.\",\n",
    "          \"..#..\",\n",
    "          \".#.#.\",\n",
    "          \"#...#\",\n",
    "          \"#...#\"],\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "ROWS, COLS = 7, 5\n",
    "N = ROWS * COLS   # 35 neurons, one per pixel\n",
    "\n",
    "def to_vector(rows):\n",
    "    # turn the text drawing into a flat +1/-1 vector\n",
    "    return np.array([1 if ch == \"#\" else -1 for row in rows for ch in row])\n",
    "\n",
    "all_patterns = {name: to_vector(rows) for name, rows in letters.items()}\n",
    "# start with 4 letters that look quite different from each other\n",
    "patterns = {k: all_patterns[k] for k in [\"C\", \"H\", \"T\", \"X\"]}\n",
    "print(\"number of neurons:\", N)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def show(vec, ax, title=\"\"):\n",
    "    ax.imshow(vec.reshape(ROWS, COLS), cmap=\"gray_r\", vmin=-1, vmax=1)\n",
    "    ax.set_title(title, fontsize=10)\n",
    "    ax.axis(\"off\")\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, len(patterns), figsize=(10, 2.5))\n",
    "for ax, (name, vec) in zip(axes, patterns.items()):\n",
    "    show(vec, ax, name)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Training\n",
    "\n",
    "Training a Hopfield network is just the Hebbian rule: \"neurons that fire together, wire together\". For every stored pattern `p` we add the outer product `p p^T` to the weight matrix. Then we divide by N and set the diagonal to 0 so a neuron doesn't connect to itself."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def train(pattern_list):\n",
    "    W = np.zeros((N, N))\n",
    "    for p in pattern_list:\n",
    "        W += np.outer(p, p)\n",
    "    W = W / N\n",
    "    np.fill_diagonal(W, 0)\n",
    "    return W\n",
    "\n",
    "W = train(list(patterns.values()))\n",
    "print(\"weight matrix shape:\", W.shape)\n",
    "print(\"symmetric?\", np.allclose(W, W.T))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Recall\n",
    "\n",
    "To recall, we start from a noisy pattern and update neurons one by one (asynchronously, in random order). Each neuron looks at the weighted sum of all the others and flips to +1 or -1 to match the sign. We keep going until a full pass changes nothing, which means the network has settled into a stable state.\n",
    "\n",
    "I also compute the energy `E = -1/2 * s^T W s`. Every update can only keep the energy the same or lower it, so the network always ends up in some minimum."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def energy(W, s):\n",
    "    return -0.5 * s @ W @ s\n",
    "\n",
    "def recall(W, start, max_sweeps=20):\n",
    "    s = start.copy()\n",
    "    energies = [energy(W, s)]\n",
    "    for sweep in range(max_sweeps):\n",
    "        changed = False\n",
    "        for i in np.random.permutation(N):\n",
    "            new_val = 1 if W[i] @ s >= 0 else -1\n",
    "            if new_val != s[i]:\n",
    "                s[i] = new_val\n",
    "                changed = True\n",
    "        energies.append(energy(W, s))\n",
    "        if not changed:\n",
    "            break\n",
    "    return s, energies\n",
    "\n",
    "def add_noise(vec, flip_fraction):\n",
    "    noisy = vec.copy()\n",
    "    n_flip = int(round(flip_fraction * N))\n",
    "    idx = np.random.choice(N, n_flip, replace=False)\n",
    "    noisy[idx] *= -1\n",
    "    return noisy"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Trying it out\n",
    "\n",
    "Flip about 15% of the pixels in each letter and see what the network gives back."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "noise_level = 0.15\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(3, len(patterns), figsize=(10, 7))\n",
    "for col, (name, original) in enumerate(patterns.items()):\n",
    "    noisy = add_noise(original, noise_level)\n",
    "    result, _ = recall(W, noisy)\n",
    "    show(original, axes[0, col], f\"original {name}\")\n",
    "    show(noisy, axes[1, col], \"noisy\")\n",
    "    ok = np.array_equal(result, original)\n",
    "    show(result, axes[2, col], \"recalled\" + (\" \u2713\" if ok else \" \u2717\"))\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Energy going down\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's look at one recall in detail and plot how the energy changes after each sweep."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "noisy_T = add_noise(patterns[\"T\"], 0.2)\n",
    "result, energies = recall(W, noisy_T)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.plot(energies, marker=\"o\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"sweep\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"energy\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Energy while recalling a noisy T\")\n",
    "plt.grid(alpha=0.3)\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"start energy:\", round(energies[0], 3))\n",
    "print(\"final energy:\", round(energies[-1], 3))\n",
    "print(\"energy of the clean T:\", round(energy(W, patterns[\"T\"]), 3))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. How much noise can it take?\n",
    "\n",
    "For each noise level from 0% to 50%, I corrupt every letter 100 times and count how often the network gets the exact original letter back."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "noise_levels = np.arange(0, 0.55, 0.05)\n",
    "trials = 100\n",
    "accuracy = []\n",
    "\n",
    "for nl in noise_levels:\n",
    "    correct = 0\n",
    "    total = 0\n",
    "    for name, original in patterns.items():\n",
    "        for _ in range(trials):\n",
    "            result, _ = recall(W, add_noise(original, nl))\n",
    "            correct += np.array_equal(result, original)\n",
    "            total += 1\n",
    "    accuracy.append(correct / total * 100)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.plot(noise_levels * 100, accuracy, marker=\"o\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"noise (% of pixels flipped)\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"correct recall (%)\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Recall accuracy vs noise\")\n",
    "plt.grid(alpha=0.3)\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "for nl, acc in zip(noise_levels, accuracy):\n",
    "    print(f\"{nl*100:4.0f}% noise -> {acc:5.1f}% correct\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 7. How many letters can it store?\n",
    "\n",
    "A Hopfield network can't store unlimited patterns. The rough rule is about `0.14 * N` patterns, which for 35 neurons is only around 5. Past that the stored patterns start interfering with each other and the network settles into wrong \"spurious\" states.\n",
    "\n",
    "To test this I use random +1/-1 patterns (since I only have 6 letters), store `k` of them, add 10% noise, and check recall."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def capacity_test(k, noise=0.1, runs=30):\n",
    "    correct = 0\n",
    "    total = 0\n",
    "    for _ in range(runs):\n",
    "        pats = [np.random.choice([-1, 1], N) for _ in range(k)]\n",
    "        Wk = train(pats)\n",
    "        for p in pats:\n",
    "            result, _ = recall(Wk, add_noise(p, noise))\n",
    "            correct += np.array_equal(result, p)\n",
    "            total += 1\n",
    "    return correct / total * 100\n",
    "\n",
    "ks = list(range(1, 13))\n",
    "cap = [capacity_test(k) for k in ks]\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.plot(ks, cap, marker=\"o\")\n",
    "plt.axvline(0.14 * N, color=\"red\", linestyle=\"--\", label=\"0.14 * N\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"number of stored patterns\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"correct recall (%)\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Storage capacity (N = 35)\")\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.grid(alpha=0.3)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 8. What happens if I add the similar letters?\n",
    "\n",
    "I drew 6 letters but only stored 4 so far. A looks a lot like H, and L looks a lot like C. Let me store all 6 and compare recall at 15% noise with the 4-letter version."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "W6 = train(list(all_patterns.values()))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"letter  correct recall (15% noise)   |   4 letters   6 letters\")\n",
    "for name, original in all_patterns.items():\n",
    "    hits4 = hits6 = 0\n",
    "    for _ in range(200):\n",
    "        noisy = add_noise(original, 0.15)\n",
    "        if name in patterns:\n",
    "            hits4 += np.array_equal(recall(W, noisy)[0], original)\n",
    "        hits6 += np.array_equal(recall(W6, noisy)[0], original)\n",
    "    a = f\"{hits4 / 200 * 100:5.1f}%\" if name in patterns else \"  n/a \"\n",
    "    print(f\"  {name}                                      {a}      {hits6 / 200 * 100:5.1f}%\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(\"overlap between letters (1 = identical, -1 = opposite):\")\n",
    "names = list(all_patterns)\n",
    "print(\"   \", names)\n",
    "for a in names:\n",
    "    print(a, [round(float(all_patterns[a] @ all_patterns[b]) / N, 2) for b in names])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "- A Hopfield network can restore a letter from a noisy version using only a weight matrix built from the Hebbian rule.\n",
    "- Recall works well with small amounts of noise and falls apart as noise approaches 50% (at that point the input looks more like random pixels than a letter).\n",
    "- Storage capacity is limited to roughly 0.14 * N patterns. Beyond that, recall gets much worse.\n",
    "- Similar patterns (high overlap, like A/H and C/L) are harder to tell apart. When I stored all 6 letters, H and L stopped being recalled at all, even though the other 4 letters kept working with the smaller set.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Things I could try next:** a bigger grid (more neurons means more capacity), the 8x8 digits dataset from scikit-learn, or modern Hopfield networks which can store far more patterns."
   ]
  }
